WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.840
A cell holds many proteins.

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:07.580
How can oxygen make it destroy HIF-1α and spare the rest?

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.340
Watch the target, not the tag.

4
00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.380
Yellow ubiquitin is reusable.

5
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.620
Grey proteins are possible targets.

6
00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.400
Green E3 is the selector.

7
00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:27.530
E1 spends ATP to activate ubiquitin, then holds it temporarily.

8
00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:31.730
E2 receives and carries that activated tag.

9
00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:36.770
E3 brings loaded E2 beside a target it recognizes.

10
00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:41.010
Ubiquitin usually lands on a target lysine.

11
00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:45.750
The tag is common; recognition makes the transfer selective.

12
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.340
The same relay can serve many targets because the selector can change.

13
00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.820
Different E3 receptors fit different target features.

14
00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.600
E1 and E2 keep ubiquitin moving.

15
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.120
The E3 receptor decides which grey block enters the green grip.

16
00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:09.610
A degron is the target feature an E3 recognizes.

17
00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:14.250
It can be a short sequence, a chemical change, or both.

18
00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:18.670
Signals can create that feature or strengthen its contact.

19
00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:24.170
Signals change protein fate by changing what an E3 can recognize.

20
00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:27.430
Now follow oxygen into this rule.

21
00:01:28.433 --> 00:01:33.213
HIF-1α is made continuously in the cells pictured here.

22
00:01:34.393 --> 00:01:39.633
When oxygen is available, a prolyl hydroxylase uses it as a reaction substrate.

23
00:01:40.953 --> 00:01:45.833
The enzyme adds a hydroxyl group to a proline on HIF-1α.

24
00:01:46.593 --> 00:01:48.953
That small badge completes a degron.

25
00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:52.813
Here are two HIF-1α targets.

26
00:01:54.053 --> 00:01:56.953
One carries the hydroxyl badge, and one does not.

27
00:01:58.213 --> 00:01:59.833
Which target will VHL grip?

28
00:02:05.817 --> 00:02:09.237
VHL closes around the hydroxylated target.

29
00:02:10.517 --> 00:02:15.437
A peptide with hydroxyproline bound VHL tightly without cell extract.

30
00:02:16.757 --> 00:02:19.497
The same peptide with ordinary proline did not.

31
00:02:19.497 --> 00:02:23.997
The modification itself supplies the recognized feature.

32
00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:30.640
Once VHL holds HIF-1α, the ligase builds a ubiquitin chain.

33
00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.580
A red proteasome accepts the marked target and breaks it down.

34
00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:39.920
New HIF-1α keeps entering the system.

35
00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:43.780
With oxygen, removal keeps its level low.

36
00:02:44.883 --> 00:02:49.403
When oxygen falls, the hydroxylase completes its reaction less often.

37
00:02:50.743 --> 00:02:53.903
Fewer HIF-1α molecules gain the badge.

38
00:02:55.303 --> 00:02:58.623
VHL finds fewer targets, while synthesis continues.

39
00:03:00.223 --> 00:03:06.423
HIF-1α accumulates, pairs with ARNT, and helps switch on low-oxygen genes.

40
00:03:07.433 --> 00:03:09.993
Notice what changed and what stayed shared.

41
00:03:11.313 --> 00:03:14.333
Oxygen changed the target feature VHL could read.

42
00:03:15.513 --> 00:03:18.633
It did not invent a new ubiquitin or proteasome.

43
00:03:19.953 --> 00:03:23.993
Signals change protein fate by changing what an E3 can recognize.
